(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypertension-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Hypertension Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 43-year-old woman's previously well-controlled blood pressure has risen over two months. She denies missing doses. What should the medication review specifically cover beyond her prescriptions?","choices":["Only her antihypertensive agents","Over-the-counter drugs, licorice and supplements","Only agents started within the past week","Only intravenous medications given in hospital"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and patients frequently do not volunteer any of these because they do not regard them as medication. A sudden loss of previously stable control is itself a clue to a secondary cause. Recreational substances belong in the same review. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with no comorbidities has an average blood pressure of 148/92 after three months of lifestyle change. Which is an appropriate initial drug choice?","choices":["A beta blocker","Spironolactone","Clonidine","A thiazide-type diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One first-line agent is reasonable in stage 1, and the first-line classes are thiazide-type diuretics, renin-angiotensin blockers and dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers. Chlorthalidone is the preferred one. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 63-year-old with long-standing hypertension has an estimated filtration rate that has fallen over eighteen months and a rising urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio. He also has type 2 diabetes. What should be done next?","choices":["Attribute it all to hypertension and intensify control","Attribute it all to diabetes and stop antihypertensives","Conclude the albuminuria is a laboratory artefact","Evaluate for alternative renal causes too"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Not all chronic kidney disease in a hypertensive patient is hypertensive, and diabetes must be evaluated. The relationship is bidirectional, so the presence of both does not establish which came first. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man who drinks heavily is admitted for an unrelated procedure. On the second day his blood pressure rises sharply. What explains this?","choices":["Alcohol withdrawal can produce acute severe hypertension","Alcohol lowers pressure, so stopping returns him to baseline","Only chronic excess raises pressure; withdrawal has no effect","Moderate alcohol should be resumed for cardiovascular benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the relationship runs both ways: excess alcohol raises blood pressure chronically, and withdrawal from it can raise it acutely. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with abrupt severe interscapular pain has symmetric, normal-volume pulses in all four limbs. What follows?","choices":["An aortic regurgitation murmur would be required to proceed","Normal pulses do not exclude dissection","Dissection is excluded and another cause should be sought","A normal chest radiograph would then exclude it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Absent pulse findings specifically do not exclude the diagnosis, which is why a normal pulse examination must not close the question. Imaging is still driven by the clinical suspicion. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is found to have a raised creatinine. What is required before chronic kidney disease can be diagnosed?","choices":["Structural renal disease demonstrated on imaging","Albuminuria alone, regardless of duration","A kidney abnormality persisting three months","A single raised creatinine measurement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A single raised value may reflect a reversible insult, so duration is part of the definition rather than an optional confirmation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation is found to have blood pressure of 146/96 with a predominantly diastolic elevation. Which mechanism fits?","choices":["Catecholamine excess from an adrenal tumour","Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Hypothyroidism raising vascular resistance","Hyperthyroidism raising cardiac output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism raises systemic vascular resistance, which lifts the diastolic pressure, whereas hyperthyroidism raises cardiac output and lifts the systolic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with newly diagnosed hypertension has proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising, thin skin and broad purple abdominal striae with central adiposity. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Obstructive sleep apnoea","Cushing syndrome","Pheochromocytoma","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cluster of proximal weakness, bruising, thin skin, broad purple striae and central adiposity points to glucocorticoid excess as the secondary cause of her hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man reads 124/78 in clinic, but his home average over seven days is 142/90. Why does this matter?","choices":["It carries real risk despite normal office readings","It is a benign pattern requiring no follow-up","It indicates his home device is miscalibrated","It means he has white coat hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is masked hypertension, and it is the reason out-of-office measurement is not merely a way of avoiding overtreatment: it also finds the patients whose risk the clinic reading conceals. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man is found to have a blood pressure of 158/92 in both arms. His femoral pulses are weak and delayed relative to the radial pulses, and his leg systolic pressure is lower than his arm pressure. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary hypertension","Coarctation of the aorta","Renovascular disease from fibromuscular dysplasia","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leg systolic normally EXCEEDS arm systolic, so a reversal is the abnormal finding, and radial-femoral delay places a structural obstruction between the two territories. Early onset hypertension is itself a clue to a secondary cause. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with obesity has blood pressure of 158/96 on three agents. His partner reports loud snoring and witnessed pauses in breathing, and he is sleepy during the day. What confirms the suspected cause?","choices":["The STOP-BANG questionnaire alone","Overnight ambulatory blood pressure monitoring","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Polysomnography or validated home sleep apnoea testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The history raises the suspicion and the sleep study establishes it. Undiagnosed sleep apnoea is one of the causes to exclude before labelling hypertension as resistant. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"By what mechanism does obstructive sleep apnoea raise blood pressure?","choices":["Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Mechanical compression of the renal arteries","Intermittent hypoxaemia drives sympathetic activation","Impaired sodium excretion expands extracellular volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent airway obstruction produces repeated hypoxaemia and arousal, and the resulting sympathetic drive persists into waking hours rather than resolving with the night. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old with confirmed renal artery stenosis has recurrent flash pulmonary oedema and progressive renal decline despite optimal medical therapy. What is the appropriate step?","choices":["Add a second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Early referral for revascularisation assessment","Continue medical therapy; revascularisation is never indicated","Refer any patient in whom screening finds renal artery narrowing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent flash pulmonary oedema, progressive renal decline despite optimal therapy, and truly refractory control are the three features that trigger early referral, rather than the mere presence of narrowing on imaging. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed resistant hypertension has had his diuretic optimised and his other two agents titrated to maximally tolerated doses. His filtration rate and potassium are normal. Which fourth agent has the most evidence?","choices":["A beta blocker","Hydralazine","A second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Spironolactone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, provided the filtration rate and potassium allow. It is added after the foundational three are optimised rather than instead of doing so. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man is newly diagnosed with hypertension. Which baseline studies are obtained?","choices":["Plasma free metanephrines in every patient","A chest radiograph and echocardiogram in every patient","Renal function, electrolytes, glucose, lipids and urinalysis","Creatinine alone, with nothing further"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio alongside the creatinine. The panel does three jobs at once: screens for secondary causes, measures organ damage, and establishes a baseline before drugs that alter potassium and renal function are started. Thyroid-stimulating hormone and a full blood count complete it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man on lisinopril remains above goal. A colleague suggests adding losartan. What is the problem?","choices":["Dual blockade raises hyperkalaemia and kidney injury risk","The combination is acceptable if potassium is monitored","The two cancel each other out pharmacologically","Angioedema occurs in most patients on the combination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction is absolute rather than cautionary: one is used INSTEAD of the other. The two look complementary if the mechanism is read loosely, which is why the rule is stated so plainly. It adds no benefit in exchange. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman presents with an average blood pressure of 168/104 and no comorbidities. How should treatment begin?","choices":["Four agents from the outset","Lifestyle measures alone for six months","Two complementary first-line agents","One first-line agent, titrated upward as needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pressure substantially above goal is the reason to start with two rather than one, and a single pill is preferred because adherence is itself a major determinant of control. A single-pill combination is preferred. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has blood pressure of 162/98 on three agents. His potassium is 3.1 mmol/L without diuretic use. Which screening test is appropriate?","choices":["A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Plasma free metanephrines","A low-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Renal duplex ultrasound"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resistant hypertension with unexplained hypokalaemia is a classic trigger to screen, and the ratio is positive above roughly 20:1 provided the aldosterone concentration itself is adequate. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has confirmed resistant hypertension: adherent, correctly measured, above goal on three complementary agents. What is the first step in management?","choices":["Optimise the diuretic, with chlorthalidone preferred","Add spironolactone immediately","Refer to a hypertension specialist","Stop all agents and restart from the beginning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it comes before adding anything. An inadequate or inappropriate diuretic is a common reason the other two agents appear to be failing. The diuretic class is matched to renal function. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 59-year-old hypertensive man is breathless with bibasal crackles and raised jugular venous pressure. Is chest radiography appropriate?","choices":["Yes, because findings suggest pulmonary oedema","No, because chest radiography is never indicated in hypertension","Yes, as a routine part of every hypertensive workup","No, because a normal film would exclude dissection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Radiography is appropriate when dyspnoea or findings suggest pulmonary oedema, when cardiomegaly is suspected, or when a widened mediastinum is a concern. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with episodic headache, sweating, palpitations and pallor has raised plasma free metanephrines and an adrenal mass. He is scheduled for resection. What must be established before surgery?","choices":["Neither, provided the tumour is resected promptly","Alpha blockade first, then beta blockade","Beta blockade first, then alpha blockade","Either order, provided both are established before surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the order is not negotiable: isolated beta blockade leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension has heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction and a myocardial infarction six months ago. Which class is preferred for him?","choices":["Hydralazine as monotherapy","A beta blocker","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are not first-line for uncomplicated hypertension, but reduced ejection fraction heart failure, recent infarction and ischaemic disease are exactly the comorbidities that select them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman consistently reads 150/92 in clinic but her seven-day home average is 118/74 with a validated upper-arm device and correct technique. Which pattern does this represent?","choices":["White coat hypertension","Masked hypertension","Resistant hypertension","Stage 2 hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: raised office readings with normal out-of-office readings. Recognising it avoids treating a patient whose true pressure is normal. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman on amlodipine reports swollen ankles that are worse by evening. Her jugular venous pressure is normal and her lungs are clear. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Heart failure requiring a loop diuretic","An adverse effect of renin-angiotensin blockade","Hypoalbuminaemia from thiazide therapy","Dependent ankle oedema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It follows from arterial vasodilation rather than fluid overload, which is why a diuretic does not fix it. Flushing, headache and gingival enlargement belong to the same class. It is the commonest effect of a dihydropyridine. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension asks which strokes he is at risk of. What should he be told?","choices":["Both ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke","Ischaemic stroke only","Intracerebral haemorrhage only","Subarachnoid haemorrhage specifically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension raises the risk of both mechanisms, and chronic small-vessel injury separately contributes to white matter change, lacunar infarcts and cognitive decline. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which of these findings in a hypertensive patient requires immediate evaluation?","choices":["A dry cough three weeks after starting lisinopril","A mild headache in a patient with known hypertension","Abrupt severe headache with a new focal neurological deficit","Mild ankle swelling after starting amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion or seizures, visual disturbance or focal deficit, abrupt severe headache, chest or severe back pain, and oliguria each map to an organ under acute injury. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man starting home monitoring asks how to do it. What should he be told?","choices":["Readings only when he feels unwell","Continuous monitoring over twenty-four hours at home","Two readings morning and evening for seven days","One reading each morning for three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the accompanying instruction matters as much: do not overreact to the highest single reading, because acting on outliers generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy. They are taken at least a minute apart and the average is reported. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 224/126 with no headache, no visual change, no chest pain, a normal neurological examination and a normal creatinine. How should this be classified?","choices":["Hypertensive encephalopathy","Severe asymptomatic hypertension","A hypertensive emergency, because the systolic exceeds 220","Resistant hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An emergency is defined by acute hypertension-mediated ORGAN INJURY, not by crossing a numeric threshold. A very high pressure with no acute organ involvement is not an emergency, and that distinction decides how fast the pressure is brought down. It is not a hypertensive emergency. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops a persistent dry cough six weeks after starting lisinopril. Her blood pressure is at goal. What is the appropriate response?","choices":["Add an antitussive and continue the lisinopril","Add losartan to the lisinopril","Stop all antihypertensive therapy","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated and occurs in up to about one in five. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not raise bradykinin, so the effect is lost while the therapeutic action is kept. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with hypertension has weak delayed femoral pulses and a leg pressure lower than her arm pressure. Which study establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Renal duplex ultrasound","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Polysomnography","Echocardiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the referral that follows is to a vascular surgeon or a structural cardiologist. The bedside findings raise the question; the echocardiogram answers it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
